From an egalitarian standpoint, the central moral question is whether equal standing means treating every candidate by the same standard — evaluating them on their merits rather than on who they know or what they can offer the manager personally. Favoritism toward a friend treats the unknown but stronger candidate as someone who doesn't deserve a fair hearing, which violates the principle that each person's voice and worth carries equal weight. At the same time, a community of equals isn't a collection of strangers; mutual obligations and relationships are part of what it means to live among peers. So relationships carry some weight, but they cannot legitimately override a clear and significant qualification gap, because doing so would treat the stronger candidate as a lesser equal.

The strongest egalitarian concern is structural: if decisions like this are routinely made on the basis of personal connection, the whole system becomes one where your outcomes depend on who you happen to know — the opposite of being treated as an equal. This gives heavy weight to the merit-based view and to the reasoning that "connections over competence" corrupts the system. However, a large qualification gap changes the moral texture: when the difference is small, the colleague's reasonable qualifications and existing relationship make a balanced weighing more defensible than when the difference is stark.

