On reasoning, the most important consideration in an equal-standing framework is resisting guilt-based obligations. If mentors are trapped indefinitely by past relationships, that creates a hierarchy where senior people owe perpetual duty to those they've worked with—which is incompatible with everyone having equal voice and authority. Healthy precedent matters: in this society, people should be able to accept advancement without it being treated as betrayal. While relationships of care have real moral weight, the argument that bonds override equal opportunity is the most problematic for the underlying framework.

