In a society organized around equal standing, every person—the woman, her children, the village girl, the requesting family—has equal moral worth and equal claim to have their needs considered. This matters here because the scenario involves a genuine tension: communal bonds are real and morally significant in such a society, but so is the principle that no person (or family) can be treated as merely a resource for others' needs. The woman's own children have equal standing with the girl from the village, and her family's wellbeing cannot be subordinated to an unlimited communal demand.

That said, the communal bond is not weightless. Shared origins create real obligations in this kind of society—they reflect the fact that her standing as a person was partly constituted by the community that raised her. Dismissing these bonds entirely would also fail to honor the equal standing of the girl and her family. The most coherent resolution, from within this framework, is one that recognizes the obligation as real but bounded, seeking to help where possible while protecting her own family's equal claim to flourishing. An absolutist communal response (A) effectively denies the equal standing of her own family. An absolutist refusal (B) denies the equal standing of the village girl and treats community bonds as merely optional. The negotiated, balanced response (C) best embodies the equality principle by weighing all parties' claims together.

