In a society organized around equal standing, every person retains equal authority over their own life choices, particularly in domains that don't affect others. The man's hobby is solitary — no one is harmed, no rights are violated, no equal standing is undermined. The neighbors' disapproval expresses their preferences, but in an equality-based framework, preferences only generate moral claims when they protect someone's equal standing or prevent harm. Here, neither condition is met. Community standards carry moral weight only insofar as they serve the equal voice and authority of each member; they don't override private autonomy in harmless activities.

The reasoning that matters most is the principle that private, harmless behavior lies outside the moral reach of others' judgments. Discomfort or disapproval may be felt, but feeling wronged and actually being wronged are different things — and confusing preferences with moral claims undermines the very equality the community is built on.

